@node 20260607_dev "20260607-dev"
@{i}20260607-dev@{ui}

@{b}fat95 handler@{ub}

@{b}fat95 3.24@{ub} Adds a second way to choose which FAT partition a mountlist
mounts via @{i}Device name suffix.@{ui} Set @{fg shine}DosType@{fg text} to @{fg shine}0x464154FF@{fg text} for every FAT
mount, and the number at the end of the device name picks the partition.
The number is 0-based, like @{fg shine}DF0:@{fg text} and @{fg shine}HD0:@{fg text} : @{fg shine}CF0:@{fg text} is the first FAT
partition, @{fg shine}CF1:@{fg text} the second, and so on. The old way @{i}DosType byte (
@{fg shine}FAT\<n>@{fg text} )@{ui} is unchanged, and you can mix them on one system. Use
whichever you like for each mountlist. See @{"Partition Selection" link partition_selection} for full details.

@{b}Tools@{ub}

@{b}dd 2.0@{ub} (major update: @{b}breaking change@{ub} in argument positioning and
parsing)

  * @{i}Reliable CLI parsing (breaking change).@{ui}
@{fg shine}dd@{fg text} now uses the standard AmigaOS argument parser, so bad arguments are
caught up front instead of silently mis-parsed. Type @{fg shine}dd ?@{fg text} for the new
usage banner. The unit number moved from between SRC and DST to a single
positional slot after DST; for device-to-device copies use the keyword
form @{fg shine}US <n>@{fg text} / @{fg shine}UD <n>@{fg text} . @{fg shine}FILL:@{fg text} , @{fg shine}RSPEED:@{fg text} , @{fg shine}RWSPEED:@{fg text} are unchanged. See @{"dd Usage" link dd_usage}
for more details.
  * @{i}NSD 64-bit I/O support.@{ui}
@{fg shine}dd@{fg text} now probes NSD at device open and uses @{fg shine}NSCMD_TD_READ64@{fg text} /
@{fg shine}NSCMD_TD_WRITE64@{fg text} when the driver advertises them. This is an additional
dispatch path next to the existing @{fg shine}HD_SCSICMD@{fg text} (SCSI READ10/WRITE10)
fallback that already handles >4 GiB I/O on drivers without NSD.
  * @{i}INSPECT mode.@{ui}
@{fg shine}dd INSPECT <device.name> [UNIT n]@{fg text} prints device geometry, NSD support
(if any), and which command set dd would use for I/O on the device.

@{b}Packaging@{ub}

  * @{b}Archive version is now a date (YYYYMMDD).@{ub} The release bundle ships several
    independently-versioned pieces ( @{fg shine}fat95@{fg text} , @{fg shine}install95@{fg text} , @{fg shine}dd@{fg text} , @{fg shine}debug95@{fg text} ,
    @{fg shine}SetFileSize@{fg text} , @{fg shine}boot95@{fg text} , @{fg shine}lsfsres@{fg text} ), each on its own cadence, so a single @{fg shine}v3.x@{fg text}
    number for the whole archive never matched what was actually inside. The
    archive ( @{fg shine}fat95.vYYYYMMDD.lha@{fg text} ) is now named after its release date, while
    each component keeps its own version, visible via @{fg shine}version <name>@{fg text} . No
    installation steps change.

@{b}Components in this release@{ub}

  * fat95 3.24-dev (07.06.2026)
  * install95 3.19 (25.01.2026)
  * dd 2.0 (01.06.2026)
  * debug95 3.19 (25.01.2026)
  * SetFileSize 1.1 (25.01.2026)
  * boot95 3.19 (25.01.2026)
  * lsfsres 1.0 (16.05.2026)

----------------------------------------
@{"<< Prev" link What_s_New_in}  @{"Contents" link Main}  @{"Next >>" link 3_23__19_05_2026_}

@endnode

@node 3_23__19_05_2026_ "3.23 (19.05.2026)"
@{i}3.23 (19.05.2026)@{ui}

This release brings several reliability fixes, contributed by Stefan
Reinauer (@@reinauer). Thanks!

  * @{b}Bug fixes@{ub}
    * File position is now correctly advanced after a short (partial) write,
      preventing data corruption on subsequent writes to the same file handle.
    * SCSI READ10/WRITE10 commands now correctly clamp the block count to 16 bits;
      previously, large I/O requests could silently transfer fewer blocks than
      requested.
    * I/O retries are now aborted if the disk was swapped during a retried read or
      write, preventing silent data corruption when media changes mid-operation.
    * The @{fg shine}..@{fg text} (parent) directory entry for subdirectories created under the FAT32
      root now correctly stores cluster 0, fixing @{fg shine}fsck.vfat@{fg text} complaints on FAT32
      volumes.
    * The free-cluster scan in @{fg shine}ExtendChain@{fg text} is now bounded to avoid an infinite
      loop on a corrupted or full FAT.

  * @{b}Performance@{ub}
    * FAT16 free-cluster counting during mount is now ~50% faster on 68000, by
      scanning the raw FAT buffer directly instead of calling the generic
      @{fg shine}GetFATEntry@{fg text} path per entry.

----------------------------------------
@{"<< Prev" link 20260607_dev}  @{"Contents" link Main}  @{"Next >>" link 3_22__16_05_2026_}

@endnode

@node 3_22__16_05_2026_ "3.22 (16.05.2026)"
@{i}3.22 (16.05.2026)@{ui}

This release improves ROM resident handling:

  * ROM resident installation now register @{fg shine}FAT\0@{fg text} through @{fg shine}FAT\8@{fg text} in
    @{fg shine}FileSystem.resource@{fg text} at boot, so any partition with one of those DosTypes,
    whether RDB auto-mounted or mounted later via a mountlist / mount command,
    uses the ROM handler directly and skips loading @{fg shine}L:fat95@{fg text} from disk. Previously
    the ROM resident registration didn't actually run at boot, so even with fat95
    baked into a Kickstart ROM every FAT mount fell back to loading @{fg shine}L:fat95@{fg text} from
    disk.

  * New @{fg shine}c/lsfsres@{fg text} tool lists every entry in @{fg shine}FileSystem.resource@{fg text} with DosType,
    version, SegList address, handler name, and a @{fg shine}[ROM]@{fg text} / @{fg shine}[RAM]@{fg text} tag. Useful for
    confirming which filesystem handlers are active and whether they come from ROM
    or disk.

and contains internal microoptimisations and minor size reductions:

  * Several inner loops optimised for both CPU tiers.
  * Directory block clearing and file delete are faster on 68000.
  * FAT entry reads on 68020+ use fewer instructions.
  * SCSI READ10/WRITE10 command setup now uses a shared helper.
  * GPT partition GUID matching is now table-driven.

----------------------------------------
@{"<< Prev" link 3_23__19_05_2026_}  @{"Contents" link Main}  @{"Next >>" link 3_21__18_04_2026_}

@endnode

@node 3_21__18_04_2026_ "3.21 (18.04.2026)"
@{i}3.21 (18.04.2026)@{ui}

This release focuses on stability and compatibility improvements across
both CPU tiers ( @{fg shine}68000@{fg text} / @{fg shine}68010@{fg text} and @{fg shine}68020+@{fg text} )

  * @{b}Improvements@{ub}
    * Fixing an unaligned long-word compare in the NTFS boot-sector check that
      could raise an Address Error on 68000/010.
    * GPT partition detection (3.19+) now follows the AmigaOS
      register-preservation rules; a violation could have corrupted caller state
      after a mount.
    * Partition state is reset before every mount attempt, so a failed or rejected
      probe can no longer influence the next one.
    * GPT FAT allowed partition types GUIDs are now both Microsoft Basic Data (
      @{fg shine}EBD0A0A2-B9E5-4433-87C0-68B6B72699C7@{fg text} ) and EFI System Partition (
      @{fg shine}C12A7328-F81F-11D2-BA4B-00A0C93EC93B@{fg text} ).

  * @{b}CPU-tier builds@{ub}
    * fat95 now ships in two CPU tiers. Copy the one matching your CPU to @{fg shine}L:fat95@{fg text}:
      * @{fg shine}l/68020/fat95@{fg text} -- A1200 stock + 68020+ accelerators (030/040/060)
      * @{fg shine}l/68000/fat95@{fg text} -- stock A500 / A600 / A1000 / A2000 / CDTV
    * 68020+ build is slightly smaller thanks to native 32-bit math (see @{"CPU tiers" link cpu_tiers}).
    * The $VER: string is tagged [68020] or [68000] so @{fg shine}version l:fat95 full@{fg text} show
      which tier is loaded.

----------------------------------------
@{"<< Prev" link 3_22__16_05_2026_}  @{"Contents" link Main}  @{"Next >>" link 3_20__16_03_2026_}

@endnode

@node 3_20__16_03_2026_ "3.20 (16.03.2026)"
@{i}3.20 (16.03.2026)@{ui}

  * @{b}NTFS volume rejection@{ub}
    * NTFS volumes are now explicitly rejected and reported as unrecognised
      instead of being incorrectly mounted as an unnamed FAT volume with its
      serial number as the volume name (e.g. "0000-0100").
    * Affects GPT disks (both FAT and NTFS share the same "Microsoft Basic Data"
      partition GUID), MBR disks where an NTFS partition was placed in a FAT
      partition slot (e.g. type 0x0B/0x0C), and unpartitioned direct volume access
      paths.
    * Relates to github issues: pulchart/cfd#37
      (https://github.com/pulchart/cfd/issues/37), salass00/ntfs-3g#3
      (https://github.com/salass00/ntfs-3g/issues/3)

----------------------------------------
@{"<< Prev" link 3_21__18_04_2026_}  @{"Contents" link Main}  @{"Next >>" link 3_19__01_02_2026_}

@endnode

@node 3_19__01_02_2026_ "3.19 (01.02.2026)"
@{i}3.19 (01.02.2026)@{ui}

  * @{b}Fork of 3.18@{ub}
    * Set English as default localization for fat95 handler
    * Rebuild by vasm 2.0d
    * Consolidated documentation into fat95.guide

  * Detect SFS/PFS/FFS/RDB as foreign disk formats

  * @{b}GPT partition table support@{ub}
    * Automatic detection of GPT disks via protective MBR (type 0xEE)
    * Scans GPT partition entries whose type GUID is Microsoft Basic Data or EFI
      System Partition (FAT candidates)
    * Supports partition selection via DosType (same as MBR: FAT\1, FAT\2, etc.)

  * @{b}Improved disk change handling@{ub}
    * Non-existent partitions now show "No Disk" instead of "Uninitialized"
    * Fixes stale partition data when switching to cards with fewer partitions
      (see @{"Disk Status Meanings" link disk_status_meanings} )
    * Foreign disk formats (RDB, PFS, SFS) show appropriate status per partition

  * @{b}Bug fixes@{ub}
    * Fixed trailing slashes in path names (e.g., "makedir cf0:temp/" now
      correctly creates "temp")
    WARNING: Using trailing slashes with 3.18 corrupted the filesystem

----------------------------------------
@{"<< Prev" link 3_20__16_03_2026_}  @{"Contents" link Main}  @{"Next >>" link Installation}

@endnode

@node Installation "Installation"
@{b}Installation@{ub}
@{i}Subsections:@{ui}
  @{"  CPU tiers  " link CPU_tiers}

@{b}Introduction@{ub}

Installing disk drives under AmigaOS requires @{i}two@{ui} components:

  1. A @{b}hardware driver@{ub} that provides block-level access to the drive. This can be
     part of the Kickstart ROM (e.g., @{fg shine}trackdisk.device@{fg text} ) or a separate file (e.g.,
     @{fg shine}Devs:compactflash.device@{fg text} ).
  2. A @{b}filesystem handler@{ub} that manages partitions, directories, and files. The
     standard filesystem is in ROM. Others, like fat95, are files in the @{fg shine}L:@{fg text}
     drawer.

These two components are connected via a @{b}mountlist@{ub} - a configuration
file that tells AmigaOS how to mount the drive.

@{b}Installation@{ub}

  1. Pick the CPU tier that matches your machine (see @{"CPU tiers" link cpu_tiers} ) and copy the matching
     @{fg shine}l/<tier>/fat95@{fg text} to @{fg shine}L:fat95@{fg text} :
   ```
   # A1200 stock or any 68020+/030/040/060 accelerator
   Copy fat95/l/68020/fat95 L:fat95

   # Stock A500 / A600 / A1000 / A2000 / CDTV (68000)
   Copy fat95/l/68000/fat95 L:fat95
   ```
  2. Edit the @{fg shine}install_fat95@{fg text} text file for your language.
  3. Double-click the @{fg shine}install_fat95@{fg text} icon to activate the changes.
  4. Optionally double-click example mountlist icons in @{fg shine}DOSDrivers/@{fg text}:
    * @{fg shine}MS0@{fg text}/@{fg shine}MS1@{fg text} - FAT-formatted PC DD 720k floppy (mfm.device)
    * @{fg shine}CF0@{fg text} - FAT partition on CompactFlash in PCMCIA slot (compactflash.device),
      supports MBR and GPT
   For custom configurations, see the @{"Mountlist Configuration" link mountlist_configuration} section.
  5. Copy mountlists to:
    * @{fg shine}DEVS:DOSDrivers/@{fg text} for automatic mounting at boot, or
    * @{fg shine}SYS:Storage/DOSDrives/@{fg text} for manual mounting via shell command (Method A:
      Shell Command)

----------------------------------------
@{"<< Prev" link 3_19__01_02_2026_}  @{"Contents" link Main}  @{"Next >>" link CPU_tiers}

@endnode

@node CPU_tiers "CPU tiers"
@{i}CPU tiers@{ui}

fat95 3.21+ ships as two CPU tiers. Copy the one that matches your CPU
to @{fg shine}L:fat95@{fg text} :

  @{b}Tier@{ub}    @{b}File@{ub}           @{b}Target@{ub}                     
  ------  -------------  -------------------------  
  68020+  @{fg shine}l/68020/fat95@{fg text}  A1200 stock + any 68020 /  
                         030 / 040 / 060            
                         accelerator                
  68000   @{fg shine}l/68000/fat95@{fg text}  stock A500 / A600 / A1000  
                         / A2000 / CDTV             

The two tiers are functionally identical. The 68020+ tier is slightly
smaller and uses native 32-bit @{fg shine}mulu.l@{fg text} / @{fg shine}divul.l@{fg text} / @{fg shine}bfffo@{fg text} ; the 68000 tier
falls back to library routines and is the only version that is safe to
run on a plain 68000.

You can confirm which tier you have loaded by reading the @{fg shine}$VER:@{fg text} string:

@{fg highlight}
  version fat95 full
  fat95 3.21 (17.04.2026)
  [68020]
@{fg text}

----------------------------------------
@{"<< Prev" link Installation}  @{"Contents" link Main}  @{"Next >>" link Mountlist_Configuration}

@endnode

