  @{"  Complete Mountlist Example  " link Complete_Mountlist_Example}
  @{"  Method A: Shell Command  " link Method_A__Shell_Command}
  @{"  Method B: Workbench Icon  " link Method_B__Workbench_Icon}
  @{"  Method C: Auto-mount at Boot  " link Method_C__Auto_mount_at_Boot}
  @{"  Method D: OS 1.3 (MountList file)  " link Method_D__OS_1_3__MountList_file_}

----------------------------------------
@{"<< Prev" link Manual_Partition_Definition}  @{"Contents" link Main}  @{"Next >>" link Complete_Mountlist_Example}

@endnode

@node Complete_Mountlist_Example "Complete Mountlist Example"
@{i}Complete Mountlist Example@{ui}

Two options based on DosType, both mount the first FAT partition (see @{"Partition Selection" link partition_selection}).

@{fg highlight}
  CF0:
      FileSystem     = L:fat95
      Device         = compactflash.device
      Unit           = 0
      Flags          = 0
      LowCyl         = 0 /* Auto partition search */
      HighCyl        = 1
      Surfaces       = 1
      BlocksPerTrack = 1
      BlockSize      = 512
      Buffers        = 200
      BufMemType     = 1
      MaxTransfer    = 0x1FE00
      Mask           = 0xFFFFFFFE
      StackSize      = 4096
      Priority       = 5
      GlobVec        = -1
  
      /* Option A: DosType byte (`FAT\<n>`): FAT\1 = first FAT partition */
      DosType        = 0x46415401
  
      /* Option B: DosType byte (`FAT\255`): partition by device name e.g. cf0 = first FAT partition */
      DosType        = 0x464154FF
  
      Activate       = 1
@{fg text}

For devices with more than one FAT partition:

  * Option A: copy the mountlist and change @{fg shine}DosType@{fg text} to @{fg shine}0x46415402@{fg text} (FAT\2),
    @{fg shine}0x46415403@{fg text} (FAT\3), and so on, matching the table in @{"Partition Selection" link partition_selection} .
  * Option B: copy the mountlist to @{fg shine}CF1@{fg text} , @{fg shine}CF2@{fg text} , ... and keep @{fg shine}DosType = 0x464154FF@{fg text}
    . The number on the device name picks the partition.

The @{fg shine}FileSystem = l:fat95@{fg text} line stays in every copy. AmigaOS @{fg shine}Mount@{fg text} needs
it even when fat95 is already in ROM, because the @{fg shine}FileSystem.resource@{fg text}
auto-lookup only fires on the auto-mount path, not on text-file
DOSDrivers.

----------------------------------------
